 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.3,
   "dur": 4.139,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Earth's day is now twelve hours long. Here's what you'd feel."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 5.1,
   "dur": 26.161,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "At a twelve hour day, sunrise to sunset takes six hours. The ground under your feet now moves at about thirty-three hundred kilometers an hour, nearly three times the speed of sound. You weigh one point four percent less than on a still Earth, four times what you feel today. A seventy kilogram person feels like about sixty-nine."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 32.1,
   "dur": 28.821,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "But the air has a problem. The ground speeds up at once; the atmosphere is pulled along only by friction, and that takes time. For the first hours, the air is left behind. At a twelve hour day, the ground is moving through the air at about four hundred and sixty meters a second, faster than the speed of sound. Winds like that would strip the surface, and they would only be the beginning."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 61.5,
   "dur": 2.116,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "And then the oceans start to move."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 64.5,
   "dur": 1.624,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "Full video on YouTube."
  }
 ]
